    "scenarioText": "Background \nDEF Manufacturing Pvt. Ltd., a mid-sized company in India, has recently \nencountered allegations of oppression and mismanagement. Some minority \nshareholders, feeling marginalized and concerned about the company's \ndirection, have filed a petition under Section 241 of the Companies Act, 2013. \nThis section allows shareholders to approach the National Company Law \nTribunal (NCLT) if they believe that the affairs of the company are being \nconducted in a manner prejudicial to public interest, oppressive to any \nmember(s), or resulting in mismanagement. \nKey Stakeholders: \n• Mr. Rohan Kapoor (Managing Director) \n• Ms. Shalini Mehta (CFO) \n• Mr. Karan Verma (Minority Shareholder) \n• Ms. Ayesha Khan (Independent Director) \n• Ms. Priya Nair (Legal Counsel) \nIssues Raised \n1. Financial Irregularities: Minority shareholders allege that the company's \nfinancial statements have been manipulated to hide losses and inflate \nprofits. They claim this has been done to benefit the majority shareholders \nand to influence the market perception of the company's performance. \n2. Dividend Policies: Mr. Karan Verma and other minority shareholders are \nupset that the company has not declared dividends for the past two years \ndespite reporting profits. They argue that the majority shareholders, who \ncontrol the board, are using these profits for personal benefits and \nreinvestments that disproportionately benefit them. \n3. Mismanagement Allegations: Allegations include misuse of company \nfunds, improper loans to directors, and unauthorized transactions that \nhave not been approved by the board. The minority shareholders claim \n\nthese actions are detrimental to the company's interests and violate \ncorporate governance principles. \n4. Exclusion from Decision-Making: Minority shareholders feel excluded \nfrom critical decisions, such as major investments and strategic shifts. \nThey allege that the majority shareholders are making decisions that favor \ntheir interests without considering the impact on all shareholders. \nThe minority shareholders of DEF Manufacturing Pvt. Ltd. have filed a petition \nunder Section 241 of the Companies Act, 2013, alleging oppression and \nmismanagement. The board must address these concerns legally and \ntransparently to maintain corporate governance standards and protect the \ncompany's reputation. The NCLT may intervene if the allegations are \nsubstantiated, providing remedies to correct the misconduct and protect the \ninterests of all shareholders. \nBoard Meeting Discussion \nAt a recent board meeting, the following points were discussed: \n• Mr. Rohan Kapoor refuted the allegations, stating that all financial \npractices have been in compliance with the law and that no dividends \nwere declared to reinvest profits for future growth. \n• Ms. Shalini Mehta presented audited financial statements showing \ncompliance with regulatory standards, but minority shareholders \nchallenged the veracity of these statements. \n• Ms. Ayesha Khan expressed concern about the impact of these \nallegations on the company's reputation and suggested an independent \ninvestigation. \n• Ms. Priya Nair advised that the company should respond legally and \nconsider mediation to resolve the conflict.",
