    "chapterName": "Module 2 Part II - Ch 1: The Foreign Exchange Management Act (FEMA), 1999",
    "scenarioText": "Since infancy, Janardhan Kishore along with his younger brothers Hrishikesh \nKishore and Anant Kishore had an unmatched liking for automobiles. Janardhan \nKishore’s father Roop Kishore, in partnership with his brother-in-law Shyamal, \nwas engaged in the business of pharmaceutical dealerships and therefore, the \nthree brothers knew the in-and-out of business comparatively better than \nothers. \nAfter completing their graduation in engineering, the trio thought of \nmanufacturing automotive components which would offer a diverse range of \nsafety-critical systems. The ‘manufacturing’ idea got strong support from their \nfather, Roop Kishore, and all of them ventured to incorporate a company by the \nname Robust Auto Limited. Abhishek, a cousin of Janardhan Kishore who was a \npracticing Chartered Accountant, helped in forming the company which was \nduly registered with the Registrar of Companies, Delhi and Haryana in May \n2001, and its registered office was situated in Gurugram, Haryana. The \nAuthorised Capital of the company was ` 60.00 crore divided into 6 crore equity \nshares of ` 10 each whereas the paid-up capital continues to be ` 55.00 crore \n(divided into 5.50 crore equity shares of ` 10 each). \nWith Roop Kishore at the helm of the company as Chairman and Managing \nDirector (CMD), the management team of Robust Auto Limited consisted of \nfollowing persons: \nS. \nNo. \nName Designation \n1. Roop Kishore Chairman and Managing Director (CMD) \n2. Janardhan Kishore Whole-time Director (WTD) \n3. Hrishikesh Kishore Director (Marketing) \n4. Abhishek Director (Finance and Accounts) \n5. Anant Kishore \nDirector (Human Resources and other \nMiscellaneous Departments) \n6. Madhulika Director (Production) \n7. Deeksha Company Secretary \n\n36 \n\n8. Astrid Independent Director \n9. Vasant Independent Director \nRobust Auto Limited started manufacturing and offered a wide range of safety-\ncritical systems and other engineering solutions for two-wheelers, three-\nwheelers, four-wheelers, agri-vehicles and passenger and commercial vehicles. \nThe products included automotive sheet metal and casting parts, polymer \ncomponents and mirror systems for above stated vehicles. Expanding further, \nthe company’s product portfolio also included metal chassis systems, polymer \ncomponents, suspension systems, body-in-white components, exhaust systems, \netc. With the passage of time, Robust Auto Limited has started servicing twelve \nOEMs globally. It has established around twenty manufacturing facilities across \nfifteen major cities in eleven states of the country. It is considered a \ndistinguished market leader in the high- growth field of precision sheet metal \npressing and fabrication within the growing automotive component industry. \nWith a view to significantly minimise the time period involved in manufacturing \nof automotive components, it was decided by the directors of Robust Auto \nLimited to import a ‘sophisticated machine’ from Synclair Auto Components \nLtd. of United Kingdom for GBP 60,000. After finalisation of negotiations, the \nsame was shipped in the mid of November 2024 and was received in India in \nthe last week of December 2024. Thereafter, it was installed with the help of \ntwo engineers deputed by Synclair Auto Components Ltd. at the factory site \nlocated at Gurugram. The payment of GBP 60,000 was duly made by the end of \nJanuary 2025. \nSuniti, CEO at Rachna Aircrafts Limited, New Delhi, was well known to Janardhan \nKishore from the college days. Once it so happened that Suniti consulted \nJanardhan Kishore in connection with sending of three aircraft engines for \noverhauling to Astra AeroPlanes Maintenance Limited of United Kingdom (UK) \nand re-importing them. Janardhan Kishore requested his friend Arthur who was \nlooking after overhauling and repairs department at Astra AeroPlanes \nMaintenance Limited to carry out overhauling of three aircraft engines as early \nas possible and send them back to India. Janardhan Kishore knew that Rachna \nAircrafts Limited would not be required to furnish a declaration in one of the \nspecified forms set out in the Foreign Exchange Management (Export of Goods \nand Services) Regulations, 2015, if the said aircraft engines were re-imported \n\ninto India after overhauling within the specified period from the date of their \nexport to U.K. \nBelieving in a prudent investment strategy, Robust Auto Limited held around \nsix percent of equity shares of Star Leather Products Limited which has gone \npublic with Initial Public Offer (IPO) of 85 crore equity shares of ` 10 each \namounting to ` 850.00 crore to rope in requisite funds for expansion of existing \nbusiness. It is to be noted that the company has also opted to allot equity shares \nin excess of the equity shares offered in the public issue as a post-listing price \nstabilizing mechanism. \nStar Leather Products Limited, besides producing goods for the domestic \nconsumption, also exports its various leather products to certain African \ncountries prominent of which are Nigeria, Ghana, Kenya, Algeria and South \nAfrica. In addition, exports are also made to a few European countries like \nSlovakia, Sweden, United Kingdom, France and Germany. It has also established \nwarehouses in Sweden, Kenya and South Africa with the permission of Reserve \nBank of India. Recently, Star Leather Products Limited exported a full cargo of \nits products worth USD one million to its warehouse located at Sweden. \nKeeping in view the success of Star Leather Products Limited, whose Initial \nPublic Offer (IPO) on Main Board was oversubscribed (more than ten times), the \ndirectors of Robust Auto Limited are also planning to go for Initial Public Offer \n(IPO) on Main Board in near future. Deeksha has been given the charge to work \nout the modus operandi involved in launching a public offer for an issue size of \n` 80.00 crore at the best suited time.",
